# What caused me to look into this...

wasn't being a seasoned developer myself, but seeing all the wonderful studios I love, making games I just can't run or run poorly. It started with Overwatch 2's and Marvel Rivals' launches; both saw either a hit in performance on a capable 3050 laptop or non-competitive frame rates. Marvel Rivals was especially egregious given their forced UE5 TAA temporal anti-aliasing, being a rendering technique that smooths jagged edges and reduces shimmering by blending the current frame with data from previous frames. The anti-aliasing solution that looked like my screen was evenly spread with a coat of Vaseline, since TAA basically upscales based on the last rendered frame, y'know before anything moved, then blurs to make up the difference. Even forcing off in the engine.ini made the game look another flavor of terrible with the dithering)\
![red dead redemption blurry. TAA on vs off](./render-pipeline-optimization/i2p41flb8q8c1.png)

---

# Games look bad, so what can I do?

I'm not a graphics developer, but given I study CS, I can surely look into this. After all, I would like to make a game in the near future. So, after months of research, primarily from UE forums and YouTube videos like those from Threat Interactive, I've concluded that the problem isn't that games are taxing; it's that the crutches given by UE and GPU manufacturers are so easy and simple to use, that developers got complacent, and rightfully so. Just like how we use the gym to train muscles and writing the brain, we find that there's a new type of atrophy we must train against: critical and innovative solutions as opposed to vibe coding or relying on built-in machine fixes. \
\
Again, I'm not trying to undermine developers here, just pointing out a trap all of us have fallen into: relying on something external *too* much. In my opinion, I think the best way to tackle this is to provide hard evidence that a specific rendering method is optimized or unoptimized for this particular game + scene + target hardware. But we all know if it is or isn't unoptimized. The question then becomes what and why this is unoptimized, and what the fix for it is.\
\
LLMs are too unpredictable (and we're all tired of GPT wrappers), and current frame analysis tools need experts to weigh in, so what we can do is have an algorithm that, with enough data, can determine what's rendered properly per pass, and what's not.

---

# So I built it, fast

The thing that actually pushed me over the edge was watching Red Dead 2 or Arkham Knight look better than games with twice the budget running on much better hardware, and then a hackathon in March [abdullah: which hackathon?] gave me an excuse, which was really only a push to get something done fast. The idea was to launch the game through Nvidia's Nsight, grab GPU traces, check each render pass against a budget (shadows shouldn't eat more than X% of a frame in a game like yours) and let an LLM write up the findings (yes, the GPT wrapper I complained about two paragraphs ago).\
\
Almost nothing launched. Out of the nine games I pointed it at, MOTORSLICE (a demo I had lying around) was the first one that even opened under Nsight, so it became the entire test suite, all 27 traces and 3.3 GB of it.\
\
It also lied to me. The parser never got a single pass timing out of the Nsight exports, so the budget checker saw 0.0 ms of work and gave every capture an A, and then the LLM took that empty scoresheet and wrote a thousand words anyway, and one of those reports diagnosed an RTX 4060 with 8 GB. This laptop has a 3050 with 4, and every prompt in my logs says so. [abdullah: this is the March 7 story from the chat. keep or cut]\
\
The capture itself froze the game for five to ten seconds. That's fine if all you want is a screenshot, but it's useless for catching a stutter, since by the time the capture starts the stutter is long gone and all you have is one frozen frame out of the whole session.

---

# Take two, FramePilot

In June I started over, this time as two tools instead of one.\
\
The sentinel watches every frame from outside the game. Intel's PresentMon reads the same Windows event stream the frame counters use, so nothing gets injected into the game (anti-cheat doesn't even notice) and the game never knows it's there. Its only job is deciding when something is wrong, whether that's a spike, a sustained drop, or the CPU holding the GPU up.\
\
The surgeon is the other half, and it only does anything once the sentinel says so. One RenderDoc capture while things are bad, and then it goes through what's in it, which passes ran, how many draws each one made, and what resolution the game is really rendering at underneath the upscaler.\
\
March actually already asked what kind of game you were making (open world, corridor, VR, competitive or cinematic), but the percentages behind each answer were numbers I made up, and a made-up number with a letter grade on it is the same nonsense minus the LLM. An expensive pass isn't automatically an unoptimized one either, a GI pass eating 30% of the frame is normal for an open world but there's no reason a corridor shooter should be paying that. So this version only hands out a grade against a well-optimized game of the same kind, measured on the same machine, which is also why it hasn't graded anything yet (see the end).

![FramePilot, the desktop app](./render-pipeline-optimization/framepilot-gui.png)

> (the desktop version. it scans your Steam library, guesses the engine, and marks the games it can use as a known-good reference. Half-Life 2 gets a tick.)

First real session, 11 June, 131,354 frames of MOTORSLICE.

![frame time across the whole session, stutters marked](./render-pipeline-optimization/session1-frametimes.png)

> (every frame the sentinel saw. the rule behind the cluster of dots at the start is dumb on purpose: four or more stutters with most of them inside the first 90 seconds and it calls it shader compilation. here 11 of the 17 landed in the first 45 seconds, so it did, and it probably was, the game had just started.)

This is the report it wrote for that session, trimmed:

```
# Performance report — MOTORSLICE Demo
Session 1 · 2026-06-11 10:38 · target 60 fps · NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 Laptop GPU

## Framerate
- Average 91 fps (10.9ms) · median 92 fps
- 1% low 55 fps · 0.1% low 24 fps · worst frame 491ms
- GPU-bound 95% of frames, avg GPU-busy 10.9ms

## Findings
### 1. Shader/PSO compilation stutter  CRITICAL · confidence high
- 11 of 17 stutters occurred in the first 45s of the session (10:39:08–10:39:53).
- Worst spike at 10:39:29: 188ms frame (20x baseline).
- Stutters fade once the session warms up — consistent with first-use
  compilation rather than a scene cost.
- Developer: Enable PSO precaching / bundled PSO caches so pipeline states
  compile at load, not on first use (UE: r.PSOPrecaching=1, ship a precache list).
```

> (every line has to point at a number with a timestamp behind it, that was the one rule I gave the report writer.) [abdullah: the real report follows the framerate block with a line saying the 1% lows may read worse than reality because that build only stored a sample of frames. a raw recompute over all the frames gives 45.9 / 12.6. quoting as-is is fine, just so you know it's there]

