# Reading the game's own stopwatch

My GPU gives RenderDoc nothing when you ask it how long each pass took (no counters on this laptop), so the surgeon could see what a frame was made of but had no idea what any of it cost, which is kind of the whole point.\
\
What it can see is the game's own stopwatch. Unreal brackets every pass with timestamp queries (shipping builds included) and copies them into small ring buffers every frame, and those buffers are sitting inside the capture file. So instead of asking the GPU for new timings, I read the ones the game already took.

```py
"""Recover per-pass GPU timing from a game's own D3D12 timestamp queries.

THE HARD PART (researched against a real MOTORSLICE capture)
  * There are many query heaps (one per parallel command list / queue).
  * Each heap's ring holds several frames of history, so consecutive
    timestamps include large cross-frame / idle gaps (5-16 ms) mixed with
    real intra-pass deltas (< 1 ms).
  * Heaps are offset on the shared GPU clock; the captured frame is slowed
    by RenderDoc, so absolute tick sums are meaningless.
"""

def robust_delta_cap(deltas, k=5.0):
    """Upper bound separating intra-pass deltas from cross-frame/idle gaps.
    median + k * MAD: needs no clock frequency, tolerates a heavy pass or two."""
    pos = [d for d in deltas if d > 0]
    if not pos:
        return 0.0
    med = _median(pos)
    mad = _median([abs(d - med) for d in pos])
    if mad == 0:
        return max(med * 3.0, med + 1)
    return med + k * mad
```

> (median plus MAD is just there to throw away the idle gaps the docstring is on about. I still haven't checked these numbers against a real profiler, so the tool only reports them as a share of the frame and doesn't grade off them.)

