# What one frame of MOTORSLICE looks like

```
pass                                     conf    draws  indirect
Shadow depths (atlas)                    high      753       251
Occlusion culling (hardware queries)     high      672         0
GBuffer / base pass                      high      339        83
Depth pre-pass                           high      264        62
Shadow depths (atlas)                    high      112        38
Scene color / lighting+translucency      medium     75        21
```

865 draws into shadow maps against 354 in the main view, rendering at 768x576 and upscaling to 720p (so 48% of the pixels), and 22 separate full-screen passes on top of that. So yeah, that's the Vaseline from the top of this post, just in numbers now.

---

# My own tool stuttered the game

Every capture writes a file somewhere between 260 and 450 MB, and the game stalls for about a second while it does. My own sentinel flagged that stall as a critical stutter, in the same session I was using to look for stutters, while I was sitting right there watching it.\
\
The stats now know when the tool caused the hitch and leave those frames out, but it did make me pause to think if this approach was wrong in the first place, since the tool has to hurt the frame to measure it.

---

# Where it stands (September 2026)

It's paused for now.\
\
349 tests pass (against fake IO, so they prove the logic, not the pipeline) and the whole thing has run end to end on one game. The per-pass timings have never been checked against a real profiler. Zero of the 21 known-good reference buckets are recorded (Portal 2, Days Gone, DOOM 2016, Metro Exodus Enhanced Edition and so on, I picked the list but never got around to capturing any of them). The knowledge base has 16 entries and I wanted 40.\
\
My skills and knowledge just weren't complete enough to finish it, so I stepped back to gain more knowledge and also focus on work, and that ate the rest of the summer. [abdullah: nearly your words, minus the "vibe code everything" part since you said not to mention how it was built]\
\
I still think the idea holds up though. With the right fundamental info about specific engines and current trends (TAA, upscaling everything), you can easily optimize your game to look and run way better, and the tool is mainly there so devs and gamers can understand what and why their game looks so bad.
